body:
Root cause seems to be that internally, the update does a `DELETE` followed by an `INSERT` on the same row in the index and the error happens because the insert does not see the delete.
Looks like a simpler repro (on `1.3`) is:
```
postgres=# create table test(a int primary key, b int);
CREATE TABLE
postgres=# begin transaction;
BEGIN
postgres=# insert into test values (1);
INSERT 0 1
postgres=# delete from test;
DELETE 1
postgres=# insert into test values (1);
ERROR:  duplicate key value violates unique constraint "test_pkey"
```

The commit https://github.com/YugaByte/yugabyte-db/commit/ca3ebdb6cf12b11af2c52d6ba8fd682c2a80ed5b fixed one issue of the bug.  The second issue in this bug must be also fixed.

(1) The issue that was fixed. Delete behaves correctly for the following case.
create table tab(i int primary key);
insert into tab values(1);
begin;
delete from tab;
end;

(2) The issue that was NOT fixed. Delete behaves incorrectly for the following case.
create table tab(i int primary key);
begin;
insert into tab values(1);
delete from tab;
end;

issue.body:
Updating and deleting data also share similar errors.
This bug is originally reported in #1611 

Test case
```
CREATE TABLE read_time_test(i INT, t TEXT, j INT UNIQUE, k INT, PRIMARY KEY(i, j));
BEGIN;
INSERT INTO read_time_test VALUES (1, 'one', 1), (2, 'two', 2), (3, 'three', 3), (4, 'four', 4);
DELETE FROM read_time_test WHERE i = 1;
INSERT INTO read_time_test VALUES (1, 'one', 1, 1);
INSERT INTO read_time_test VALUES (11, 'one', 11, 11);
SELECT * FROM read_time_test ORDER BY i;
DELETE FROM read_time_test WHERE i < 10;
INSERT INTO read_time_test VALUES (1, 'one', 1), (2, 'two', 2), (3, 'three', 3), (4, 'four', 4);
SELECT * FROM read_time_test ORDER BY i, t;
UPDATE read_time_test SET k = 20;
UPDATE read_time_test SET k = 30 WHERE i < 11;
UPDATE read_time_test SET k = 40 WHERE i = 11;
SELECT * FROM read_time_test ORDER BY i, t;
END;
SELECT * FROM read_time_test ORDER BY i, t;
```
